# Reticulum mesh bridge — environment and settings

Both files are untracked. `.env` holds secrets (identity hashes count as
secrets here: the repo is public).

## Delivery settings

    # auto (default) | off | a 32-char hex destination hash
    RETICULUM_PROPAGATION_NODE=auto

Read through `extra -> scoped env -> default`, so it can also be set per
platform in config.yaml under the reticulum adapter's `extra` block, which
wins over this file.

Values:

- `auto` — leave LXMF autopeering enabled (max depth 4). The node picks a
  propagation node from those it syncs with.
- `off` — disable autopeering and clear any pinned node. A reply with no path
  to the peer fails instead of parking on a third party.
- `<32 hex>` — pin that node. Set this when you run your own propagation node
  or want to name a trusted one; autopeering gives no say over which node
  carries your traffic.

An invalid value raises at startup rather than silently changing policy.

## Access control

    RETICULUM_DISPLAY_NAME=<name shown on the mesh>
    HERMES_RETICULUM_ALLOW_ALL=false
    HERMES_RETICULUM_ALLOWED_USERS=<comma-separated destination hashes>

With `ALLOW_ALL=false`, only listed hashes are admitted. Uppercase and
colon-separated spellings of the same hash both match.

## Announce cadence

    RETICULUM_ANNOUNCE_INTERVAL=60      # minutes, default 60

Leave this at 60. The mesh is shared, and a gateway announcing more often than
the operator's own client is poor etiquette. A cold path is handled by
requesting a path and waiting, and by propagating, not by announcing harder.
